title: 'Three RE2/Perl regex divergences survive karr #161, all measured'
updated: 2026-08-21T10:35:31Z
---

docs/adr/0048 closed 28 of the 29 measured (rule, key) disagreements between unencrypted_regex/encrypted_regex as Perl matches them and as RE2 matches them. Three shapes survive and are recorded as Limits there rather than fixed:

1. FULL CASE FOLDING. Perl (?i) folds U+00DF to "ss" and RE2 does simple
   folding only. Measured: `unencrypted_regex: (?i)^ss$` over a key U+00DF --
   sops ENCRYPTS the value, we leave it READABLE. That is the secret-bare
   direction and it is the one row of the 43 still divergent after #161.
   Perl has no simple-fold-only flag: /aa suppresses it and suppresses the
   k/U+212A and s/U+017F folds RE2 DOES perform along with it (measured both
   ways), so /aa is one-for-two in the wrong direction.

2. $ BEFORE A TRAILING NEWLINE. Perl $ is (?=\n?\z), RE2 $ is \z. Measured:
   key "foo\n" (a legal double-quoted YAML key) under `unencrypted_regex:
   ^foo$` -- sops encrypts, we leave bare. Not refused, on purpose: ^foo$ is
   a pattern sops takes and reads back, so ADR 0038 discriminator says
   refusing it is wrong. Any fix has to rewrite the pattern ($ -> \z outside
   a class, outside an escape, not under (?m)), which is a mini-parser on the
   rule text.

3. \p{NAME} WITH A NAME GO DOES NOT HAVE. Measured through the .sops.yaml
   path_regex oracle: \p{Word}, \p{Alpha}, \p{IsAlpha}, \p{XPosixAlpha} are
   RE2 "invalid character class range" -- so the rule matches nothing at sops
   -- while \p{Greek} and \p{Lu} are accepted by both. #161 accepts \p and \P
   unconditionally rather than refuse \p{Greek}, which sops takes. An exact
   fix needs Go unicode category and script table on this side.

None of the three is reachable by an ASCII rule over ASCII keys.
